        <div id="runner" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Tru Grit Runner Curved Manual Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            The Grit Runner is a self-powered curved treadmill: no motor, no cord, and a belt that moves as fast
            as you push it. Running higher up the front of the curve speeds the belt up; drifting back slows it.
            What makes it different is the resistance knob. Dealers consistently describe six levels, with 1 and
            2 for running, 3 and 4 for walking (where more resistance helps the belt feel steady under a slow
            stride) and 5 and 6 for sled pushes, where you lean into the handrails and drive the belt like a sled.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What is confirmed</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>330 lb maximum user, on every dealer listing we read.</li>
            <li>Steel and aluminium frame.</li>
            <li>Battery-powered LCD with time, distance, pace, speed, calories and interval modes.</li>
            <li>Warranty: lifetime frame; 2 years on the resistance belt, the tread and the monitor.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What is not</h3>
          <p>
            Tru Grit's dealers do not publish the running-surface size or machine weight. Garage Gym Reviews, as
            shown in search results, gives a 17 by 60-inch running surface, and our <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill roundup</a> found the size and weight varying between sources. Garage Gym Reviews' warranty notes also appear to
            give a lifetime belt term in one place and two years in another. If belt length matters to you — it
            does on a curved treadmill, because the length of the curve decides how much room you have to change
            pace — ask the dealer to measure or quote it from the manual before you order.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What reviewers say</h3>
          <p>
            Independent coverage is mixed. Garage Gym Reviews scored it in the mid-3s out of 5. One independent
            review our roundup cited criticised wide gaps between slats and the general finish, and another found
            it loud during sprints. Owners buying it for conditioning work tend to accept that; runners who want a
            refined running feel tend to spend more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="runner-vs-elite" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Grit Runner vs. Grit Runner Elite</h2>
<div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4"></th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Grit Runner</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Grit Runner Elite</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Resistance</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6 levels (run, walk, sled push)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4 magnetic levels, dual-belt transmission</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Battery LCD</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">LCD with Bluetooth and ANT+</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Max user</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">330 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not published</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Machine weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Varies by source</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">291 lb (TreadmillReviews.com)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Warranty</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime frame; 2 years belt, tread, monitor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dealers disagree (see card)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Price, 5 Nov 2026</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,996 to $3,299</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,495</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The Elite is the more refined machine on paper. TreadmillReviews.com describes a gentler curve, a
            cushioned slat belt, a more solid build and better interval modes, and dealers list sealed bearings,
            aluminium roller guides and transport wheels. Its console connects to apps — dealers name Kinomap and <a href="/zwift-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Zwift</a> — which the standard Runner's cannot.
          </p>
          <p>
            The problem is the price gap. At $3,495 the Elite is up against the AssaultRunner Pro and TrueForm
            Trainer, both of which publish a weight rating (350 lb and 400 lb), have far more independent coverage
            and come from companies with working websites. Unless the Elite's magnetic resistance is what you are
            paying for, those are the safer purchases. The standard Runner, by contrast, has no close rival at its
            price with sled settings, which is why it is the Tru Grit we would consider.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-and-dealers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Warranty, Support and Where to Buy</h2>
<p>
            Because Tru Grit's own site was not reachable on the day we checked, the dealer you buy from matters
            more than usual. Select Fitness's listing says it is an authorised dealer, that the manufacturer's
            warranty applies, that coverage can vary by use (residential, light commercial or commercial), and
            that claims go to Select Fitness's own support team. The Fitness Outlet describes itself as an
            authorised Tru Grit dealer selling new equipment with manufacturer warranties. That is the
            arrangement you want: a dealer that will take your call if a slat or the monitor fails.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Before you order</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Ask the dealer who handles a warranty claim, and get the warranty terms in writing.</li>
            <li>Ask whether the dealer stocks spare slats, the resistance belt and the monitor.</li>
            <li>Check the returns policy: Select Fitness listed a 30-day return window for unused items, with
            return shipping not refunded, which on a curved treadmill is a large freight bill.</li>
            <li>For the Elite, ask for the maximum user weight in writing.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a> explains what each term covers, and our <a href="/treadmill-delivery-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">delivery guide</a> covers freight
            delivery of a heavy machine.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">On Amazon</h3>
          <p>
            The standard Grit Runner has an Amazon listing titled "Tru Grit Fitness Runner Curved Manual -
            Treadmill … 6 Resistance Levels, LCD", which our product pipeline matched on 4 November 2026; the
            Grit Runner card's button goes straight to it. Check who the seller is and what warranty they offer
            before buying there. We did not find an Amazon listing we could tie to the Elite.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="curved-for-you" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Is a Curved Manual Treadmill Right for You?</h2>
<p>
            Tru Grit only makes curved manual treadmills, so the first question is whether you want one at all.
            They are excellent for sprints, intervals, HIIT and sled-style conditioning, need no outlet, and
            encourage an upright, forefoot running style. They are hard work for long easy runs and have no
            incline, so they suit athletes who already run outdoors better than beginners.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are unsure, read our <a href="/manual-vs-motorized-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual
            vs. motorized treadmill guide</a> and our <a href="/curved-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill guide</a>. For
            sessions built around the sled settings, see <a href="/curved-treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill workouts</a>;
            for technique, <a href="/how-to-use-a-manual-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to use a
            manual treadmill</a>. If you want a manual treadmill for walking only, flat-belt models in our <a href="/best-manual-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best manual treadmills</a> roundup
            cost a fraction of a Grit Runner.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Other curved treadmills with resistance</h3>
          <p>
            The <a href="/bells-of-steel-blitz-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bells of Steel Blitz</a> has six magnetic levels and a 400 lb rating at $4,749.99, a better-documented option if your budget
            stretches. The Centr x HYROX Perform Tread, the official HYROX race treadmill, has eight magnetic
            levels and a sled-push handlebar at $6,999. For the curved treadmills without resistance, the <a href="/trueform-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">TrueForm</a> and <a href="/woodway-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Woodway</a> guides cover the
            premium end.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Tru Grit Treadmill</h2>
          <p>The Grit Runner’s price is its attraction. These are the ways buyers end up regretting it.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying from the first dealer you find</h3>
              <p>The same Grit Runner was listed from $1,996 to $3,299 on one day. Compare at least three dealers, and include the freight charge and returns terms in the comparison.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Not asking who handles the warranty</h3>
              <p>With Tru Grit’s own website unreachable when we checked, the dealer is your route to a claim. Choose one that says in writing that it handles warranty service, and ask whether it stocks slats and monitors.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying the Elite without a weight rating</h3>
              <p>Tru Grit does not publish the Elite’s maximum user weight. At $3,495 you can buy an AssaultRunner Pro rated for 350 lb or a TrueForm Trainer rated for 400 lb. Get the Elite’s figure in writing before ordering if you are heavy.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Expecting a refined running feel</h3>
              <p>Independent reviews criticise the standard Runner’s slat gaps, finish and noise at a sprint. It is a conditioning tool at a low price. If smooth daily running matters most, spend more or look at the SOLE C80.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Tru Grit makes two curved manual treadmills: the Grit Runner, $1,996 to
          $3,299 at dealers, and the Grit Runner Elite at $3,495.</strong> The standard Runner is one of the
          cheapest curved treadmills with real sled-push settings and a 330 lb rating, and a reasonable buy from
          a dealer that will handle warranty claims, given that Tru Grit's own website was not working when we
          checked.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Elite is harder to recommend: at the same price the <a href="/assaultrunner-pro-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">AssaultRunner Pro</a> and <a href="/trueform-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">TrueForm Trainer</a> publish weight ratings and have far more independent coverage. Near the standard Runner's price,
          compare the SOLE C80 first.
          </p>
        </div>

